# /commit $ARGUMENTS

Create a **standardized commit** for `{{manifest.project.name}}`. Commits are the project's
**long-term memory** (Golden Rule 3) — `/prime` reads `git log` to reconstruct what was built and
what comes next, so consistency matters. **Thin frontend** over
`~/.agentic-workflows/piv-loop-runbook.md` (Phase 4). Operates under
`~/.claude/rules/ecc/common/golden-rules.md`.

> **Graceful degradation.** This is plain `git` — no optional tooling required.

## Guardrails (hard limits)

- Commit only — **never push, never force-push, never tag a release** from here.
- **No secrets.** Refuse to stage `.env`, key files, or anything matching a secret pattern; abort
  and report if found.
- **No `Co-Authored-By` trailer** unless this project's `.claude/settings.json` sets
  `attribution.commit` (per repo policy). The Bash tool's default template may suggest one — omit it.

## Algorithm

1. `git status` + `git diff --staged` (and unstaged) to see what changed. If nothing is staged,
   stage the intended changes (`git add -A` for the current work, excluding secrets/build artifacts).
2. **Scan for secrets** in the staged set; abort if any are found.
3. **Compose a conventional message**: `<type>: <description>` where type ∈
   `feat | fix | refactor | docs | test | chore | perf | ci`. Use `$ARGUMENTS` as scope/hint when
   given. Keep the subject imperative and ≤ ~72 chars; add a short body only when it adds signal
   (what + why, not how). One commit = one coherent change (typically one PIV phase).
4. `git commit` with that message. Print the resulting `git log --oneline -1`.

## Notes

- Customize per project (Golden Rule 2): tighten the type set, subject style, or body template to
  match your conventions — the value is that every commit looks the same.
- After committing, the PIV loop continues: evolve the AI layer if needed, then `/prime` the next phase.
